            <!-- SECTION 6.2 -->
            <section id="sec6-2" class="content-card bg-white dark:bg-slate-800 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 shadow-sm scroll-mt-20">
                <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 pb-4 mb-6">
                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-neb-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Section 6.2</span>
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-1">Relation Between Linear and Angular Quantities</h2>
                </div>

                <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                    <!-- Derivation Box 1: v = r \omega -->
                    <div class="derivation-box p-5 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/80 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-neb-600 space-y-3">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-base">Derivation 1: Relation between Linear Velocity ($v$) and Angular Velocity ($\omega$)</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm">
                            Let a body move along a circle of radius $r$. If it covers an arc distance $\Delta s$ in time $\Delta t$, the subtended angular displacement is:
                        </p>
                        <div class="text-center font-mono text-sm py-1">
                            $$\Delta \theta = \frac{\Delta s}{r} \implies \Delta s = r \cdot \Delta \theta$$
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm">
                            Dividing both sides by $\Delta t$ and taking the limit as $\Delta t \to 0$:
                        </p>
                        <div class="text-center font-mono text-sm py-1">
                            $$\lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\Delta s}{\Delta t} = r \left( \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\Delta \theta}{\Delta t} \right)$$
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm">Since $v = \frac{ds}{dt}$ and $\omega = \frac{d\theta}{dt}$, we get:</p>
                        <div class="bg-neb-100 dark:bg-slate-800 text-neb-900 dark:text-neb-200 font-bold text-center py-2 rounded-lg text-base">
                            $$v = r\omega \quad \text{or in vector form: } \vec{v} = \vec{\omega} \times \vec{r}$$
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Derivation Box 2: Total Acceleration -->
                    <div class="derivation-box p-5 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/80 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-neb-600 space-y-3">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-base">Total Linear Acceleration in Non-Uniform Circular Motion</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm">
                            In general circular motion, the velocity vector $\vec{v}$ changes both in <em>magnitude</em> and in <em>direction</em>. Therefore, the net acceleration $\vec{a}$ has two perpendicular components:
                        </p>
                        <ol class="list-disc list-inside text-sm space-y-2 pl-2">
                            <li>
                                <strong>Tangential Acceleration ($a_t$):</strong> Responsible for changing the magnitude of linear velocity ($v$).
                                <span class="font-mono text-xs block pl-6 my-1">$a_t = \frac{dv}{dt} = \frac{d(r\omega)}{dt} = r \frac{d\omega}{dt} = r\alpha$</span>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <strong>Radial / Centripetal Acceleration ($a_c$):</strong> Responsible for changing the direction of velocity (directed towards the center).
                                <span class="font-mono text-xs block pl-6 my-1">$a_c = \frac{v^2}{r} = \omega^2 r$</span>
                            </li>
                        </ol>

                        <p class="text-sm mt-3">
                            Since $\vec{a}_t \perp \vec{a}_c$, the <strong>magnitude of total acceleration ($\vec{a}$)</strong> is:
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-slate-200 dark:bg-slate-800 p-3 rounded-lg text-center font-mono font-bold text-sm">
                            $$a = \sqrt{a_t^2 + a_c^2} = \sqrt{(r\alpha)^2 + (\omega^2 r)^2}$$
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm">
                            The direction $\phi$ with the radius vector is given by: $\tan\phi = \frac{a_t}{a_c}$.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

