                                <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white uppercase tracking-wider text-xs">Geometric Derivation Steps:</h4>
                                <p>1. Consider a particle moving with uniform speed $v$ on a circle of radius $r$.</p>
                                <p>2. At time $t$, let it be at $P_1$ with velocity $\vec{v}_1$. At time $t + \Delta t$, it reaches $P_2$ with velocity $\vec{v}_2$, where $|\vec{v}_1| = |\vec{v}_2| = v$.</p>
                                <p>3. The change in velocity is $\Delta \vec{v} = \vec{v}_2 - \vec{v}_1$.</p>
                                <p>4. The position triangle $\Delta O P_1 P_2$ and the velocity triangle are <strong>similar triangles</strong> because both are isosceles with vertex angle $\Delta \theta$.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
