                                            <!-- Normal Force N perpendicular -->
                                            <line x1="0" y1="-20" x2="0" y2="-70" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2.5" />
                                            <polygon points="0,-76 -4,-66 4,-66" fill="#10b981" />
                                            <text x="8" y="-60" font-size="11" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold">N</text>

                                            <!-- Friction Force f down slope -->
                                            <line x1="-35" y1="20" x2="20" y2="20" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="2.5" />
                                            <polygon points="26,20 16,16 16,24" fill="#f59e0b" />
                                            <text x="5" y="35" font-size="11" fill="#f59e0b" font-weight="bold">f</text>
                                        </g>

                                        <!-- Weight mg straight down -->
                                        <line x1="150" y1="130" x2="150" y2="190" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2" />
                                        <polygon points="150,195 146,186 154,186" fill="#ef4444" />
                                        <text x="158" y="190" font-size="11" fill="#ef4444" font-weight="bold">mg</text>
                                    </svg>
                                </div>

                                <div class="w-full md:w-1/2 space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
                                    <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white uppercase">1. Frictionless Road ($\mu = 0$):</h4>
                                    <p>$N \cos\theta = mg \quad \text{and} \quad N \sin\theta = \frac{mv^2}{r}$</p>
                                    <div class="font-mono text-neb-600 dark:text-neb-400 font-bold">
                                        $$\tan\theta = \frac{v^2}{rg} \implies v_o = \sqrt{rg \tan\theta}$$
                                    </div>
                                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-500">($v_o$ is optimum design speed without wear and tear).</p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Full Derivation with Friction -->
                        <div class="derivation-box p-5 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/80 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-neb-600 space-y-3">
                            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-base">Derivation for Maximum Safe Speed ($v_{\text{max}}$) with Friction ($\mu$)</h3>
                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm">
                                When a vehicle turns at maximum speed $v_{\text{max}}$, it tends to skid outwards, causing static friction $f = \mu N$ to act <strong>downwards along the incline</strong>.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm">Resolving forces vertically and horizontally:</p>

                            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono my-2">
                                <div class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-800 p-3 rounded border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">
                                    <strong>Vertical Equilibrium:</strong><br>
                                    $N \cos\theta = mg + f \sin\theta$<br>
                                    $mg = N \cos\theta - \mu N \sin\theta \quad \text{--- (1)}$
                                </div>
                                <div class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-800 p-3 rounded border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">
                                    <strong>Horizontal Centripetal Force:</strong><br>
                                    $N \sin\theta + f \cos\theta = \frac{m v_{\text{max}}^2}{r}$<br>
                                    $\frac{m v_{\text{max}}^2}{r} = N \sin\theta + \mu N \cos\theta \quad \text{--- (2)}$
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm">Dividing Equation (2) by Equation (1):</p>
                            <div class="font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-center py-1">
                                $$\frac{\frac{m v_{\text{max}}^2}{r}}{mg} = \frac{N(\sin\theta + \mu \cos\theta)}{N(\cos\theta - \mu \sin\theta)}$$
                            </div>

                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm">Dividing numerator and denominator by $\cos\theta$:</p>
                            <div class="bg-neb-100 dark:bg-slate-800 text-neb-900 dark:text-neb-200 font-bold text-center py-3 rounded-lg text-sm sm:text-base">
                                $$v_{\text{max}} = \sqrt{r g \left( \frac{\mu + \tan\theta}{1 - \mu \tan\theta} \right)}$$
                            </div>

                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm mt-3">Similarly, for minimum safe velocity $v_{\text{min}}$ (to avoid sliding down when moving too slow):</p>
                            <div class="bg-slate-200 dark:bg-slate-800 font-bold text-center py-2 rounded-lg font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm">
                                $$v_{\text{min}} = \sqrt{r g \left( \frac{\tan\theta - \mu}{1 + \mu \tan\theta} \right)}$$
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

