                            When a cyclist leans inward by angle $\theta$, the normal reaction $R$ tilts. Its horizontal component ($R \sin\theta$) provides necessary centripetal force for the turn, while the vertical component ($R \cos\theta$) balances weight $mg$. This prevents the turning torque of friction from toppling the cyclist outward.
